#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""peer-host — WHICH MACHINE is a given Claude Code peer session running on.

    peer-host <selector> [--json] [--strict] [--host all|workbench|laptop]

Prints ONE host label (`workbench` / `laptop`) on stdout and exits 0. Nothing
else goes to stdout, ever — diagnostics are stderr — so a caller can do:

    host=$(peer-host datapacket-talos-ef) || exit   # route work to $host


🔴 THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES, AND WHY THE OBVIOUS SOURCE IS THE WRONG ONE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The live peer listing (the harness's own agent list) renders rows like

    datapacket-talos-ef [ef79ae] · interactive · idle · tmux scratch2:@62.%62 · 38m

— name, ref, kind, state, tmux coordinates, age, and NO HOST. A session reading
that listing cannot tell which machine a peer is on, so it cannot route work to
the right one. Session names derive from the REPO, which is orthogonal to the
machine, and tmux window names collide across hosts.

The brief pointed at `session-manager`, which does claim the cross-host tmux
view. Measured on 2026-09-12, it cannot answer this question, for a STRUCTURAL
reason and not a coverage one:

  * `session-manager` never reads the harness session registry at all
    (`grep -c 'claude/sessions' scripts/session-manager` -> 0). Its rows carry
    `session`, `window_id`, `label`, `claude_session_id` … and no harness peer
    NAME. There is nothing in its output to match a peer name against, on any
    host, so no amount of coverage would make the lookup possible.
  * Its `claude_session_id` is the only field that could bridge to a registry
    record, and its coverage is asymmetric where it matters most: measured in
    one `--json --no-ch` run, **laptop 7 of 31 windows, workbench 50 of 53**.
    Even at 100% it would still need the registry to map name -> sessionId.

So this tool reads the registries directly, one per host, which is both the
complete source (49/49 local, 9/9 laptop in that same measurement) and the
cheap one — no tmux scan, no ClickHouse, one SSH round trip.


🔴 THE REGISTRY IS PER-HOST, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM
-------------------------------------------------------------
Records live at `~/.claude/sessions/<pid>.json` on the machine the session runs
on. There is no host dimension in that path — which is exactly WHY the listing
has no host column, and also exactly what makes the answer derivable: a record's
host is the host whose filesystem it was found on. Nothing is inferred from the
name, and there is no static name->host map to go stale. Start a session on
either box and it is answerable immediately, with no file edited anywhere.

`session-resolve` reads the same registry and documents the same fact, but only
for the LOCAL host (its `PRESENCE_EXCLUDED` state is precisely "this window is
on another machine, so it can never carry a record here"). This tool is the
cross-host half of that: it reads every peer's registry, local one off disk and
the rest over SSH.


🔴 THE REMOTE LEG SELF-IDENTIFIES — a wrong address cannot be mislabelled
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
`10.42.0.100` is the LAPTOP; `10.42.0.10` is the homelab GATEWAY. An SSH leg
pointed at the wrong one does not fail: it succeeds against a real host and
reports that host's state under the label we asked for. A constant alone cannot
catch that, because the constant is the thing that would be wrong.

So the remote program **computes its own label** — using the very same
`scripts/lib/host_label.py` source, shipped over stdin rather than reimplemented
— and prints it in a sentinel line. If the answer is not the label we dialled,
the leg is REFUSED (`label_mismatch`) rather than recorded. One rule, executed
on both machines, and a positive control on which machine answered.

The sentinel is also the difference between "answered with nothing" and "the
command was swallowed": stdout with no sentinel is `no_sentinel`, never an empty
success. (Same vocabulary as `session-manager`'s `_host_ledger`.)


🔴 NEVER THE LOCAL HOST AS A DEFAULT
--------------------------------------
The dangerous failure for a routing caller is answering "workbench" for a laptop
session because the laptop probe failed. It cannot happen here: a host that was
not searched contributes NO matches, so a laptop-only name yields ZERO matches,
and zero matches with an unsearched host is `incomplete` (exit 4) — a distinct
code from `unmatched` (exit 3), which means every host really was read and the
name really is not live anywhere.

When a UNIQUE match IS found while another host went unread, the answer is a
positive measurement rather than a default, so it is returned (exit 0) with a
loud stderr warning; `--strict` turns that case into exit 4 as well.


🔴 THE BRACKETED `[ref]` IS NOT SUPPORTED, AND IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM HERE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two independent sweeps failed to derive it: the brief's (11 candidate registry
inputs x 6 hash algorithms x 3 offsets x 48 records) and this one's (9 inputs
including the raw UUID bytes, both byte orders, and `bridgeSessionId`, x 11
algorithms including CRC32/Adler32, against the one ground-truth
`(name=datapacket-talos-ef, ref=ef79ae)` pair). Zero hits.

An observation that is NOT built on: in that single pair the ref's first two
characters equal the name's suffix (`ef79ae` / `…-ef`). n=1, and 1-in-256 by
chance, so it is recorded here and used for nothing. Guessing a ref->peer rule
off one sample would answer the WRONG HOST silently, which is the one outcome
this tool exists to prevent. Pass the NAME (or a session id, pid or tmux
address) instead; `--json` echoes which key matched.


SELECTOR KINDS (a superset of the brief's "name or ref")
---------------------------------------------------------
  name         exact match on the registry `name` — the string the listing shows
  session_id   full session id, or a hex prefix of >= MIN_SESSION_ID_PREFIX
  pid          the process id, exactly
  tmux         `session:@window.%pane`, or the `session:@window` prefix

Every kind is tried and the MATCHES ARE UNIONED, so one record hit by two kinds
is still one match. `tmux` is absent from many records (all 9 laptop records
carried none when measured) and a record with no address simply cannot match
that kind — it is not an error and it does not exclude the record from the
others.


EXIT CODES
----------
  0  resolved    exactly one live peer matched; its host is on stdout
  2  ambiguous   more than one live peer matched (candidates on stderr)
  3  unmatched   zero matched, and EVERY in-scope host was read successfully
  4  incomplete  zero matched but a host could not be read — or, under
                 --strict, a unique match while a host could not be read
 64  usage       bad arguments (EX_USAGE; deliberately NOT 2, so a caller can
                 never read a typo'd flag as "ambiguous")
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

_LIB = str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "lib")
if _LIB not in sys.path:
    sys.path.insert(0, _LIB)
import host_label as _hl  # noqa: E402

HOST_NAMES = _hl.HOST_NAMES
HOST_ALL = "all"

DEFAULT_REGISTRY_DIR = "~/.claude/sessions"

#: 🔴 The protocol's positive control. The remote prints this token plus the
#: label it computed FOR ITSELF; stdout without it is `no_sentinel`, never an
#: empty success. Versioned so a future payload change cannot be read by an old
#: client as a well-formed empty answer.
SENTINEL = "PEERHOST1"

SSH_OPTS = ("-o", "BatchMode=yes",
            "-o", "ConnectTimeout=5",
            "-o", "StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new")
#: The laptop answers over nebula at ~140 ms RTT, so the budget is generous
#: relative to the work (a directory of small JSON files).
DEFAULT_SSH_TIMEOUT = 25.0

#: 🔴 A session-id prefix shorter than this is refused as a session_id selector.
#: 4 hex chars over ~50 live sessions is a coin-flip for a collision, and a
#: collision here resolves to `ambiguous` at best and the wrong host at worst.
#: 6 keeps the false-match rate negligible while still being copy-pasteable.
MIN_SESSION_ID_PREFIX = 6

# --- selector kinds --------------------------------------------------------- #
KIND_NAME = "name"
KIND_SESSION_ID = "session_id"
KIND_PID = "pid"
KIND_TMUX = "tmux"
ALL_KINDS = (KIND_NAME, KIND_SESSION_ID, KIND_PID, KIND_TMUX)

# --- per-host read statuses -------------------------------------------------- #
READ_OK = "read"
READ_UNREACHABLE = "unreachable"        # the leg never answered
READ_NO_SENTINEL = "no_sentinel"        # answered, but not with our protocol
READ_LABEL_MISMATCH = "label_mismatch"  # answered as a DIFFERENT machine
READ_ERROR = "error"                    # the registry itself could not be read
READ_OUT_OF_SCOPE = "out-of-scope"      # excluded by --host
ALL_READ_STATUSES = (READ_OK, READ_UNREACHABLE, READ_NO_SENTINEL,
                     READ_LABEL_MISMATCH, READ_ERROR, READ_OUT_OF_SCOPE)

#: The only status that counts as "this host was searched". Everything else
#: leaves the host UNSEARCHED, which is what turns a zero into `incomplete`.
SEARCHED_STATUSES = (READ_OK,)

# --- results ------------------------------------------------------------------ #
STATUS_RESOLVED = "resolved"
STATUS_AMBIGUOUS = "ambiguous"
STATUS_UNMATCHED = "unmatched"
STATUS_INCOMPLETE = "incomplete"

EXIT_RESOLVED = 0
EXIT_AMBIGUOUS = 2
EXIT_UNMATCHED = 3
EXIT_INCOMPLETE = 4
#: sysexits(3) EX_USAGE. 🔴 NOT argparse's default of 2 — that is EXIT_AMBIGUOUS
#: here, and a mistyped flag reported as "ambiguous" is a wrong answer, not an
#: error message.
EXIT_USAGE = 64

_EXIT_FOR = {
    STATUS_RESOLVED: EXIT_RESOLVED,
    STATUS_AMBIGUOUS: EXIT_AMBIGUOUS,
    STATUS_UNMATCHED: EXIT_UNMATCHED,
    STATUS_INCOMPLETE: EXIT_INCOMPLETE,
}


# =========================================================================== #
# The registry reader — runs on BOTH sides of the SSH seam
# =========================================================================== #
#
# 🔴 THIS SOURCE IS SHIPPED TO THE REMOTE VERBATIM, concatenated with
# `scripts/lib/host_label.py` and piped to `python3 -`. That is why it is a
# string and not an imported function: there is exactly ONE implementation of
# "read this machine's registry and say which machine I am", and both the local
# and the remote leg execute it. A second, remote-only copy is precisely how the
# two would drift, and a drift here reads as a real difference between hosts.
#
# It must stay stdlib-only and Python-3.9-compatible, and it must not import
# anything from this repo beyond the host_label source prepended to it.
_READER_SOURCE = r'''
def _peerhost_read_registry(directory, proc_dir="/proc"):
    """[record, ...] for every live session in `directory`. Raises on OSError.

    STALE RECORDS ARE DROPPED. The file is named for the pid, so a record whose
    pid is gone describes a session that has exited; counting it could invent an
    ambiguity, or answer with a host the peer left. Measured 2026-09-12: 0 stale
    of 49 locally and 0 of 9 on the laptop — the harness prunes — so this is a
    guard, not a hot path. It is a `/proc` check, which pid reuse can only make
    too PERMISSIVE (a record kept that should have gone), never too strict, so
    it is never worse than not filtering.

    `proc_dir` is the seam that makes the drop TESTABLE — a suite cannot create
    entries under the real `/proc`, so without it every hermetic fixture record
    would look stale and the reader would return an empty list for reasons that
    have nothing to do with the code under test.
    """
    import json as _json
    import os as _os
    out, stale, unreadable = [], 0, 0
    for entry in sorted(_os.listdir(directory)):
        if not entry.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        path = _os.path.join(directory, entry)
        try:
            with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
                raw = _json.load(fh)
        except (OSError, ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            unreadable += 1
            continue
        if not isinstance(raw, dict):
            unreadable += 1
            continue
        pid = raw.get("pid")
        if not isinstance(pid, int) or isinstance(pid, bool):
            unreadable += 1
            continue
        if not _os.path.isdir(_os.path.join(proc_dir, str(pid))):
            stale += 1
            continue
        out.append({
            "name": raw.get("name"),
            "session_id": raw.get("sessionId"),
            "pid": pid,
            # 🔴 MAY BE ABSENT. All 9 laptop records carried no `tmux` field
            # when measured; a reader that assumed the key would have raised on
            # the whole host and reported the laptop as unreadable.
            "tmux": raw.get("tmux"),
            "cwd": raw.get("cwd"),
            "kind": raw.get("kind"),
            "status": raw.get("status"),
        })
    return {"records": out, "stale_dropped": stale, "unreadable": unreadable}


def _peerhost_main(directory):
    import json as _json
    import os as _os
    import sys as _sys
    label = local_host_label()
    directory = _os.path.expanduser(directory)
    try:
        payload = _peerhost_read_registry(directory)
        payload["error"] = None
    except OSError as exc:
        payload = {"records": [], "stale_dropped": 0, "unreadable": 0,
                   "error": "%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)}
    payload["dir"] = directory
    # The sentinel FIRST, on its own line, carrying the label this machine
    # computed for itself. The caller refuses the leg if either is missing.
    _sys.stdout.write("%s %s\n" % (_PEERHOST_SENTINEL, label))
    _sys.stdout.write(_json.dumps(payload) + "\n")
'''


def _reader_namespace():
    """Exec the shipped reader locally and hand back its two functions.

    🔴 THE LOCAL LEG RUNS THE SHIPPED SOURCE, not a hand-written twin. If the
    two legs ever disagree it is because the machines disagree, which is the only
    difference this tool is allowed to report.
    """
    ns = {"local_host_label": _hl.local_host_label,
          "_PEERHOST_SENTINEL": SENTINEL}
    exec(compile(_READER_SOURCE, "<peer-host reader>", "exec"), ns)  # noqa: S102
    return ns


_FUTURE_LINE = "from __future__ import annotations"


def remote_program(host_label_source: str) -> str:
    """The complete program piped to the remote `python3 -`.

    `host_label.py`'s source is PREPENDED rather than reimplemented, so
    `local_host_label()` means the same thing on both machines. Two things about
    splicing a real module body into a larger program, both of which are hard
    SyntaxErrors or silent stdout corruption rather than soft failures:

      * 🔴 `from __future__` MUST be the first statement in the compiled unit,
        so the module's own copy is stripped and ONE is emitted at the top.
        Leaving it in place makes the whole remote program a SyntaxError, which
        arrives as an unreachable-looking host.
      * 🔴 `python3 -` runs this as `__main__`, which DEFEATS the module's
        `if __name__ == "__main__"` guard — its main block prints the bare label
        and would land on stdout ahead of our payload. `__name__` is rebound
        around the spliced body so the guard holds. (`parse_leg_output` scans
        for the sentinel rather than assuming line 1, so a stray line would be
        survivable — but "survivable" is not "correct", and a future payload
        that needs line-1 framing must not inherit the mess.)
    """
    body = "\n".join(line for line in host_label_source.splitlines()
                     if line.strip() != _FUTURE_LINE)
    return "\n".join([
        _FUTURE_LINE,
        "__name__ = '_peerhost_hostlabel'",
        body,
        "__name__ = '__main__'",
        "_PEERHOST_SENTINEL = %r" % SENTINEL,
        _READER_SOURCE,
        "import sys as _s",
        "_peerhost_main(_s.argv[1] if len(_s.argv) > 1 else %r)"
        % DEFAULT_REGISTRY_DIR,
    ])


# =========================================================================== #
# Reading one host
# =========================================================================== #
def _default_runner(argv, *, input_text=None, timeout=None):
    """(returncode, stdout, stderr). argv is a LIST — never a shell string."""
    proc = subprocess.run(list(argv), input=input_text, capture_output=True,
                          text=True, timeout=timeout)
    return proc.returncode, proc.stdout, proc.stderr


def parse_leg_output(stdout: str, expected_label: str) -> dict:
    """The remote/local program's stdout -> `{status, records, …}`. PURE.

    Four outcomes, none of which collapse into "empty":
      * no sentinel line          -> no_sentinel  (the command was swallowed)
      * sentinel, WRONG label     -> label_mismatch (we dialled another machine)
      * sentinel + payload error  -> error        (the registry itself failed)
      * sentinel + payload        -> read
    """
    base = {"records": [], "stale_dropped": 0, "unreadable": 0,
            "reported_label": None, "dir": None, "error": None}
    lines = (stdout or "").splitlines()
    head = None
    body = None
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if line.startswith(SENTINEL + " "):
            head = line
            body = "\n".join(lines[i + 1:])
            break
    if head is None:
        base["status"] = READ_NO_SENTINEL
        base["error"] = "no %s sentinel in output" % SENTINEL
        return base
    reported = head[len(SENTINEL) + 1:].strip()
    base["reported_label"] = reported
    if reported != expected_label:
        base["status"] = READ_LABEL_MISMATCH
        base["error"] = (
            "dialled %r but the host answering identifies as %r — refusing to "
            "label its sessions %r" % (expected_label, reported, expected_label))
        return base
    try:
        payload = json.loads(body)
    except ValueError as exc:
        base["status"] = READ_NO_SENTINEL
        base["error"] = "sentinel present but payload unparseable: %s" % exc
        return base
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        base["status"] = READ_NO_SENTINEL
        base["error"] = "payload is %s, not an object" % type(payload).__name__
        return base
    base.update({k: payload.get(k, base.get(k))
                 for k in ("records", "stale_dropped", "unreadable", "dir")})
    if payload.get("error"):
        base["status"] = READ_ERROR
        base["error"] = payload["error"]
        base["records"] = []
        return base
    base["status"] = READ_OK
    return base


def read_host(host: str, *, local_host: str, registry_dir: str,
              runner=None, ssh_timeout=DEFAULT_SSH_TIMEOUT,
              host_label_source=None, proc_dir: str = "/proc") -> dict:
    """One host's registry -> a leg result. Never raises for a host problem."""
    runner = runner or _default_runner
    if host == local_host:
        return _read_local(host, registry_dir=registry_dir, proc_dir=proc_dir)
    try:
        target = _hl.ssh_target(host)
    except KeyError as exc:
        return {"status": READ_ERROR, "records": [], "stale_dropped": 0,
                "unreadable": 0, "reported_label": None, "dir": None,
                "error": str(exc), "via": None}
    if host_label_source is None:
        host_label_source = Path(_hl.__file__).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    program = remote_program(host_label_source)
    argv = ["ssh", *SSH_OPTS, target,
            "python3 - " + shlex.quote(registry_dir)]
    try:
        rc, out, err = runner(argv, input_text=program, timeout=ssh_timeout)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        return {"status": READ_UNREACHABLE, "records": [], "stale_dropped": 0,
                "unreadable": 0, "reported_label": None, "dir": None,
                "error": "%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc), "via": target}
    leg = parse_leg_output(out, host)
    leg["via"] = target
    # 🔴 A non-zero rc may ONLY override a `no_sentinel` verdict. A leg that
    # produced a correct sentinel and payload WAS read, whatever the shell's exit
    # status did afterwards; and a `label_mismatch` must keep its own name,
    # because "unreachable" would invite a retry against the very address that is
    # wrong. Without this narrowing, the ssh-said-non-zero branch would erase the
    # one diagnosis nobody can guess from the outside.
    if rc != 0 and leg["status"] == READ_NO_SENTINEL:
        leg["status"] = READ_UNREACHABLE
        leg["error"] = "ssh exit %d: %s" % (
            rc, (err or "").strip()[:300] or "(no stderr)")
    return leg


def _read_local(host: str, *, registry_dir: str, proc_dir: str = "/proc") -> dict:
    ns = _reader_namespace()
    directory = os.path.expanduser(registry_dir)
    base = {"records": [], "stale_dropped": 0, "unreadable": 0,
            "reported_label": host, "dir": directory, "error": None,
            "via": "local"}
    try:
        payload = ns["_peerhost_read_registry"](directory, proc_dir)
    except OSError as exc:
        base["status"] = READ_ERROR
        base["error"] = "%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)
        return base
    base.update(payload)
    base["status"] = READ_OK
    return base


# =========================================================================== #
# Matching
# =========================================================================== #
def _is_hex(text: str) -> bool:
    return bool(text) and all(c in "0123456789abcdefABCDEF-" for c in text)


def match_kinds(selector: str, record: dict) -> list:
    """Which selector kinds this record satisfies. PURE, and ORDER-STABLE.

    🔴 A record is matched by the UNION of kinds, so a selector that is both a
    valid name and a valid pid does not double-count one record — and a record
    missing the field a kind reads simply does not match THAT kind. `tmux` is the
    field that is routinely absent; see the reader's note.
    """
    sel = (selector or "").strip()
    kinds = []
    if not sel:
        return kinds

    if record.get("name") == sel:
        kinds.append(KIND_NAME)

    sid = record.get("session_id")
    if isinstance(sid, str) and sid:
        low = sel.lower()
        if low == sid.lower():
            kinds.append(KIND_SESSION_ID)
        elif (len(low) >= MIN_SESSION_ID_PREFIX and _is_hex(low)
                and sid.lower().startswith(low)):
            kinds.append(KIND_SESSION_ID)

    pid = record.get("pid")
    if isinstance(pid, int) and not isinstance(pid, bool):
        if sel.isdigit() and int(sel) == pid:
            kinds.append(KIND_PID)

    addr = record.get("tmux")
    if isinstance(addr, str) and addr:
        if sel == addr or addr.startswith(sel + "."):
            kinds.append(KIND_TMUX)

    return kinds


def find_matches(selector: str, legs: dict) -> list:
    """Every (host, record) pair the selector hits, across every leg that READ.

    A leg that did not reach READ_OK contributes nothing — which is the whole
    reason `incomplete` exists as a separate outcome: an unsearched host is
    silent, not empty.
    """
    out = []
    for host in HOST_NAMES:
        leg = legs.get(host) or {}
        if leg.get("status") not in SEARCHED_STATUSES:
            continue
        for rec in leg.get("records") or []:
            kinds = match_kinds(selector, rec)
            if kinds:
                hit = dict(rec)
                hit["host"] = host
                hit["matched_by"] = kinds
                out.append(hit)
    return out


def lookup(selector: str, *, hosts=HOST_NAMES, local_host=None,
           registry_dir=DEFAULT_REGISTRY_DIR, runner=None, strict=False,
           ssh_timeout=DEFAULT_SSH_TIMEOUT,
           host_label_source=None, proc_dir="/proc") -> dict:
    """The whole answer, as data. The CLI is a thin renderer over this."""
    local_host = local_host or _hl.local_host_label()
    in_scope = tuple(h for h in HOST_NAMES if h in hosts)

    legs = {}
    for host in HOST_NAMES:
        if host not in in_scope:
            legs[host] = {"status": READ_OUT_OF_SCOPE, "records": [],
                          "stale_dropped": 0, "unreadable": 0,
                          "reported_label": None, "dir": None,
                          "error": None, "via": None}
            continue
        legs[host] = read_host(
            host, local_host=local_host, registry_dir=registry_dir,
            runner=runner, ssh_timeout=ssh_timeout,
            host_label_source=host_label_source, proc_dir=proc_dir)

    matches = find_matches(selector, legs)
    searched = [h for h in in_scope if legs[h]["status"] in SEARCHED_STATUSES]
    unsearched = [h for h in in_scope if legs[h]["status"] not in SEARCHED_STATUSES]

    if len(matches) > 1:
        status, host = STATUS_AMBIGUOUS, None
    elif len(matches) == 1:
        if unsearched and strict:
            # 🔴 --strict: a unique match is still only unique among the hosts
            # that ANSWERED. Refusing is the caller's choice, not the default,
            # because the match itself is a positive measurement.
            status, host = STATUS_INCOMPLETE, None
        else:
            status, host = STATUS_RESOLVED, matches[0]["host"]
    elif unsearched:
        status, host = STATUS_INCOMPLETE, None
    else:
        status, host = STATUS_UNMATCHED, None

    return {
        "selector": selector,
        "status": status,
        "host": host,
        "matches": matches,
        "local_host": local_host,
        "hosts_in_scope": list(in_scope),
        "hosts_searched": searched,
        "hosts_unsearched": unsearched,
        "coverage": {h: {k: v for k, v in legs[h].items() if k != "records"}
                     for h in HOST_NAMES},
        "records_seen": {h: len(legs[h].get("records") or [])
                         for h in HOST_NAMES},
        "strict": bool(strict),
    }


# =========================================================================== #
# CLI
# =========================================================================== #
def _describe(m: dict) -> str:
    return "  %-9s %-34s %s  pid=%s  tmux=%s  [matched by %s]" % (
        m.get("host"), m.get("name"),
        (m.get("session_id") or "")[:8], m.get("pid"),
        m.get("tmux") or "-", ",".join(m.get("matched_by") or []))


def render_diagnostics(result: dict) -> str:
    """Everything a human needs, on STDERR. Never touches stdout."""
    out = []
    status = result["status"]
    sel = result["selector"]
    if status == STATUS_AMBIGUOUS:
        out.append("peer-host: %r matches %d live peers — refusing to guess:"
                   % (sel, len(result["matches"])))
        out.extend(_describe(m) for m in result["matches"])
    elif status == STATUS_UNMATCHED:
        out.append("peer-host: no live peer matches %r on %s (searched: %s)"
                   % (sel, " or ".join(result["hosts_in_scope"]),
                      ", ".join("%s=%d records" % (h, result["records_seen"][h])
                                for h in result["hosts_searched"]) or "none"))
    elif status == STATUS_INCOMPLETE:
        if result["matches"]:
            out.append("peer-host: --strict — %r resolved to %s, but %s could "
                       "not be searched, so uniqueness is UNPROVEN"
                       % (sel, result["matches"][0]["host"],
                          ", ".join(result["hosts_unsearched"])))
        else:
            out.append("peer-host: no live peer matches %r, but %s could not "
                       "be searched — this is NOT 'not found'"
                       % (sel, ", ".join(result["hosts_unsearched"])))
    elif result["hosts_unsearched"]:
        out.append("peer-host: WARNING — answered from %s, but %s could not be "
                   "searched; a same-named peer there would be invisible"
                   % (", ".join(result["hosts_searched"]),
                      ", ".join(result["hosts_unsearched"])))
    for host in result["hosts_unsearched"]:
        cov = result["coverage"][host]
        out.append("  %s: %s — %s" % (host, cov["status"],
                                      cov.get("error") or "(no detail)"))
    return "\n".join(out)


def build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="peer-host",
        description="Print which host a Claude Code peer session is running on.",
        epilog="exit: 0 resolved · 2 ambiguous · 3 unmatched · 4 incomplete "
               "· 64 usage")
    # 🔴 `%` is DOUBLED because argparse interpolates help strings itself
    # (`help % params`). An f-string that emitted a bare `%62` made every
    # invocation die in `add_argument` with "badly formed help string" — before
    # any lookup ran, so the tool was 100% dead, not degraded.
    p.add_argument("selector",
                   help=("peer name (datapacket-talos-ef) | session id or a "
                         f">={MIN_SESSION_ID_PREFIX}-char hex prefix | pid | "
                         "tmux address (scratch2:@62 or scratch2:@62.%%62)"))
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true",
                   help="full result on stdout instead of the bare label")
    p.add_argument("--strict", action="store_true",
                   help="refuse (exit 4) even a unique match when some host "
                        "could not be searched")
    p.add_argument("--host", default=HOST_ALL, choices=(HOST_ALL, *HOST_NAMES),
                   help="restrict the search (default: all)")
    p.add_argument("--local-host", default=None, choices=HOST_NAMES,
                   help="override which host is read off the local filesystem "
                        "(default: host_label.local_host_label())")
    p.add_argument("--registry-dir", default=DEFAULT_REGISTRY_DIR,
                   help="registry directory, on EVERY host (default: %(default)s)")
    p.add_argument("--ssh-timeout", type=float, default=DEFAULT_SSH_TIMEOUT)
    return p


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    parser = build_parser()
    # 🔴 argparse exits 2 on a usage error, and 2 is EXIT_AMBIGUOUS here. Remap
    # so a mistyped flag can never be read as a real answer about a peer.
    try:
        args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    except SystemExit as exc:
        # `--help` / `--version` exit 0 and must stay 0; every other argparse
        # exit is a usage error and becomes EX_USAGE.
        return 0 if exc.code == 0 else EXIT_USAGE

    hosts = HOST_NAMES if args.host == HOST_ALL else (args.host,)
    result = lookup(args.selector, hosts=hosts, local_host=args.local_host,
                    registry_dir=args.registry_dir, strict=args.strict,
                    ssh_timeout=args.ssh_timeout)

    diag = render_diagnostics(result)
    if diag:
        print(diag, file=sys.stderr)

    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(result, indent=2, sort_keys=True, default=str))
    elif result["status"] == STATUS_RESOLVED:
        # 🔴 THE ONLY THING THAT EVER REACHES STDOUT on the success path.
        print(result["host"])
    return _EXIT_FOR[result["status"]]


if __name__ == "__main__":  # pragma: no cover
    sys.exit(main())
